:title("Swift (subset) to LLVM IR compiler") ;
:description("Compiles a Swift subset to an LLVM IR module and runs it with the built-in
IR interpreter and the MetaJS handle runtime (llvm.RunJS).

The subset: top level code that runs in source order (declare before use, no
hoisting), let/var declarations with an optional type annotation and an
optional initializer (types are parsed and ignored, let is not enforced, and an
uninitialized declaration starts as nil), functions with LABELLED parameters
(func greet(name: String, from town: String) -> String, called greet(name: x,
from: y)), default parameter values, variadic parameters, inout parameters,
@autoclosure, overloading by parameter type, nested functions and nested types,
single expression bodies with an implicit return, struct and class (and actor)
with stored properties, methods and an explicit init (self and implicit
unqualified property access inside methods, construction without new; a struct
without an init gets the memberwise one), if/else (braces required), while,
repeat/while, for-in over the ranges 1...5 (closed) and 1..<5 (half-open), over
arrays and over dictionaries, an optional where clause on a for, labelled loops
with a labelled break/continue, guard, switch over values, break/continue,
closures as values with capture lists, the shorthand arguments dollar-zero and
up and trailing closure call syntax, the array higher order methods map/filter/
forEach plus append/contains/count/first/last, arrays and dictionaries with
subscript read/write and .count, tuples (labelled or not, tuple patterns in let
and in assignment, tuple return types), string interpolation, the nil-coalescing
operator a ?? b, optional chaining a?.b and a?[i], force unwrap a!, nil,
print(...) and return. Optional binding in conditions: if let / guard let /
while let (including the Swift 5.7 shorthand if let x), compound comma
conditions, if case / guard case patterns, a where clause on a binding, and
for case PAT in xs. Int arithmetic is 32 bit and / truncates; + concatenates
when a side is a String; == compares strings by value and objects by identity.
Enums with bare cases, raw values (E(rawValue:) and .rawValue) and associated
values, plus enum methods and computed properties; an enum case is an object
carrying its case name, its associated values and an __eqkey string, and == over
enum cases compares that key - which is what makes the leading-dot form
(.tails, .circle(r: 5)) work without a type checker. Computed properties and
willSet/didSet observers, static and class members, subscripts (including
multi-parameter and static ones), class inheritance with overrides, super.m() /
super.p / super.init(), is / as? / as!, protocols whose extensions carry default
implementations, extensions of a type (including Int/String/Array), nested types
reached as Outer.Inner, initializer overloading and failable initializers, and
struct VALUE semantics. switch matches PATTERNS: values, ranges, tuples, enum
cases with their associated values, bindings and a where guard, with
fallthrough.

Functions, methods, init and closures become IR functions jsf_N(env, args)
(methods and init get the instance prepended and bind both this and self, so
implicit property access resolves through js_kget/js_kset). Classes and structs
become descriptor objects with a __ctor (default property initializers) and an
init closure; construction runs __ctor then init. Method calls dispatch through
js_mcall, subscript through js_pyget/js_pyset, dictionaries through js_dict_new,
and ?? compiles to a null test branching around the right side (a phi merges).
A binding condition compiles to a nil test branching to the clause's failure
block, and a pattern to a chain of such tests. throw calls js_throw and do/catch
compiles the try body and the WHOLE catch chain to js_closures handed to js_try
(Swift has no finally; the catch closure tests each clause's patterns in turn,
runs the first that matches with its bindings live, and re-throws when none does;
an unbound catch binds `error`). try? wraps its operand in a js_try whose catch
answers nil. defer compiles the rest of
its block into a js_try whose FINALLY closure is the defer body, so it runs on
the normal path, on a return and on a throw, and nested defers unwind LIFO.
The whole program becomes jsmain(env, args) executed in order.
Conditional compilation (#if / #elseif / #else / #endif, at top level, in a
block, in a type body, in a closure body and around the cases of a switch) is
STRUCTURAL: the condition is read as raw text to the end of its line and never
evaluated, every branch is parsed like ordinary code, and only the first branch
whose condition is not literally false (or 0) is emitted - which for a real
condition such as os(macOS), canImport(Foundation) or swift(>=5.7) is always the
first branch. A declaration made under #if stays visible after the #endif.
#available and #unavailable compile to true and false; #sourceLocation /
#error / #warning / #assert are accepted and ignored, and a leading hashbang
line is skipped.

Overloaded members (two initializers, two subscripts) are stored under name,
name$1, ... and picked at the CALL SITE, where the argument labels and count are
known statically; a member read or write of a name some type COMPUTES goes
through the generated __get_x / __set_x method (js_defprop accessors never see
their receiver in a C-style emitter), and a program that computes no property
emits exactly the IR it did before.

Not in the subset (accepted structurally and reported as not implemented under
-warn-unsupported, or simply not parsed): floating point (the runtime is 32-bit
integer only), the numeric and string literal forms beyond plain decimals and a
double-quoted string with interpolation, generics (parsed and ignored), lazy
properties, property wrappers and their projected values, operator and
precedencegroup declarations, operator overloading, the bitwise and overflow
operators, and async/await.

Top level self checking programs count failures and end with exit(failures).
The same language with the same semantics is interpreted directly by
swift-interpreter.abnf.") ;


// --- main rules

// Shared token productions (languages/lib).
:include("lib/cstyle-comments.abnf") ;
:include("lib/ident.abnf") ;
:include("lib/numbers.abnf") ;
:include("lib/bools.abnf") ;

:startRule(Program) ;
:whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Top level items become statements of jsmain, emitted in source order.
Program     <~~ buildMain(takeAll()) ~~>
            = [ Hashbang ] { TopItem } ;
TopItem     = PoundIfTop | FunTop | TypeTop | UnsupTop | TopStmt ;
FunTop      <~~ push(makeFunStmt(pop())) ~~> = [ Modifiers ] FunDecl ;
TypeTop     = [ Modifiers ] ( StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl ) ;
UnsupTop    = [ Modifiers ] UnsupDecl ;
TopStmt     = Statement ;

// ----- #if / #elseif / #else / #endif conditional compilation -----
// Structural only: the condition is consumed as raw text to the end of its line
// and NOT evaluated. Every branch is parsed like ordinary code (so an unlowered
// construct in an inactive branch is still reported), and exactly one branch is
// emitted: the first whose condition is not the literal `false` / `0`, which for
// a real condition like os(macOS) or canImport(Foo) is always the FIRST branch.
// The kept branch's items are spliced into the enclosing list, so a declaration
// made under #if is visible after the #endif, as in Swift.
PoundIfTop  <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyTop { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyTop }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyTop ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyTop <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = { TopItem } ;
// A statement-level #if lives in the item list of a block (and of a switch body),
// not in Statement itself, because it can contribute more than one statement -
// and because Statement wraps exactly one popped thunk in stmtPos.
PoundIfStmt <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyStmt { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyStmt }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyStmt ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyStmt <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = { PoundIfStmt | Statement } ;
PoundIfMember <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyMember { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyMember }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyMember ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyMember <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = { Member } ;
// A #if around case labels of a switch: its branches hold labels AND statements.
PoundIfCase <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyCase { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyCase }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyCase ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyCase <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = { PoundIfCase | CaseLabel | Statement } ;
// A #if inside a closure body, whose items are the {stmt} / {expr} lambda records.
PoundIfLam  <~~ var xs = poundPick(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < xs.length; i++) { push(xs[i]) } ~~>
            = PoundIf PoundCond PoundBodyLam { PoundElseIf PoundCond PoundBodyLam }
              [ PoundElse PoundBodyLam ] PoundEndif ;
PoundBodyLam <~~ push({branch: takeAll()}) ~~> = { PoundIfLam | LamStmt } ;
// #elseif is spelled before #else on purpose: as a PEG, "#else" would match the
// prefix of "#elseif" and leave a stray `if` to be read as an if statement.
PoundIf     = "#if" ;
PoundElseIf = "#elseif" ;
PoundElse   <~~ push({pcond: "1"}) ~~> = "#else" ;
PoundEndif  = "#endif" ;
// The condition: every byte up to (not including) the end of the line, with
// whitespace skipping switched off so the run cannot cross the line break.
PoundCond   <~~ push({pcond: up.in}) ~~>
            = :whitespace() { PoundCondChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
PoundCondChar = "\x00"..."\x09" | "\x0b" | "\x0c" | "\x0e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// #sourceLocation(...) / #error(...) / #warning(...): accepted and ignored.
PoundDirective <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = PoundDirName SkipParen ;
PoundDirName = "#sourceLocation" | "#error" | "#warning" | "#assert" ;
// The same directives written inside a type body; the class builder ignores the
// empty member record they push, exactly as it ignores deinit's.
MemberDirective <~~ push({}) ~~> = PoundDirName SkipParen ;
// A #! line is only a hashbang on the FIRST line of the file, which is where
// Program matches it.
Hashbang    = "#!" :whitespace() { PoundCondChar } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;

// Declaration modifiers and attributes (@objc, @available(...), @escaping) are
// parsed and ignored.
Modifiers   = { Modifier | Attribute } ;
Attribute   = "@" Id { "." Id } [ SkipParen ] ;
Modifier    = ( "public" | "private" | "fileprivate" | "internal" | "open"
              | "final" | "static" | "mutating" | "nonmutating"
              | "override" | "lazy" | "weak" | "unowned" | "convenience"
              | "required" | "dynamic" | "indirect" | "package"
              | "nonisolated" | "distributed" | "borrowing" | "consuming" ) KwEnd ;
// `class` is a type-member modifier (class var / class func) as well as a
// declaration keyword, so it is NOT in Modifier - a greedy Modifiers would eat the
// `class` of `@objc class Cache { ... }` and leave nothing for ClassDecl. Member
// tries the nested-type alternative first and only then this list.
// A member's modifiers push a marker for the ones the type builder needs (static /
// class puts the member on the type descriptor instead of on instances); the rest
// are parsed and dropped. A marker arrives ahead of the member's own record, in
// source order, so splitMembers attaches it to the member that follows.
MemberModifiers = { MemStatic | Modifier | Attribute | ClassMod } ;
MemStatic   <~~ push({mstatic: 1}) ~~> = ( "static" | "class" ) KwEnd ;
ClassMod    = "class" KwEnd ;

// func name(label param: Type, ...) -> Ret { ... }
FunDecl     <~~ push(makeFun(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;

// A function/method/init body. Swift returns the value of a body that is a SINGLE
// expression without writing `return`, so the one-expression form is tried first;
// anything else is an ordinary block. ExprBody is ordered first on purpose - a body
// that starts with a declaration or a `return` fails it immediately.
FnBody      = ExprBody | Block ;
ExprBody    <~~ push(makeExprBody(pop())) ~~>
            = "{" Expression "}" ;
FnEffects   = { ( "throws" | "rethrows" | "async" ) KwEnd } ;
// A generic constraint clause on a function/type: where T: P, U == V (ignored).
GenWhere    = KwWhere GenConstraint { "," GenConstraint } ;
GenConstraint = Type ( ":" TypeList | "==" Type | "=" Type ) ;

ParamList   <~~ push({params: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ Param { "," Param } ] ")" ;
// A parameter is [externalLabel] internalName : Type; the last identifier binds.
// With two identifiers the first is the ARGUMENT LABEL and the second the bound
// name; with one, the label and the name are the same. The label `_` means "no
// label". The declared type is kept as text, for picking between overloads.
Param       <~~ push(makeParam(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({pid: up.in}) ~~> [ Id <~~ push({pid: up.in}) ~~> ] ":" [ ParamInout ] { ParamAttr } ParamType [ ParamVariadic ] [ "=" ParamDflt ] ;
ParamInout  <~~ push({pinout: 1}) ~~> = "inout" KwEnd ;
ParamType   <~~ push({ptype: up.in}) ~~> = Type ;
ParamVariadic <~~ push({pvar: 1}) ~~> = "..." ;
ParamDflt   <~~ push({pdflt: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
// Parameter/type attributes (@escaping, @autoclosure) take no argument list here,
// so no SkipParen: the ( ... ) that follows belongs to a function type.
ParamAttr   = "@" Id <~~ push({pattr: up.in}) ~~> { "." Id } ;

// class / struct with stored properties, methods and an explicit init.
StructDecl  <~~ push(makeStructStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwStruct Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ClassDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwClass Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The inheritance clause: for a class its first entry may be the SUPERCLASS, and
// every entry may be a protocol whose default implementations are mixed in. Each
// name is kept as text and resolved when the type is created.
InheritList = InheritName { "," InheritName } ;
InheritName <~~ push({inherit: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// An actor is a class with an isolation domain. There is no executor in this
// runtime (async functions are defined, never awaited), so it lowers like a class.
ActorDecl   <~~ push(makeClassStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwActor Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
KwActor     = "actor" KwEnd ;
TypeList    = Type { "," Type } ;

// A nested type is matched BEFORE the modifier-led members, so the `class` of
// `class Inner {}` is read as the declaration keyword and the `class` of
// `class var kind` as a modifier (ClassDecl fails on the `var` that follows).
Member      = PoundIfMember | MemberDirective | MemberType | [ MemberModifiers ] ( CaseDecl | InitDecl | DeinitDecl | SubscriptDecl | MethodDecl | ComputedProp | PropDecl ) ;
// The nested declaration's own statement is dropped: a nested type is accepted
// structurally, not yet reachable as Outer.Inner.
MemberType  <~~ push({nested: pop()}) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] ( StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl | TypeAliasDecl ) ;
InitDecl    <~~ push({method: makeInit(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwInit [ "?" | "!" ] [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;
// deinit and subscript are accepted structurally, not implemented; a class/struct
// builder ignores the {} marker they push.
// deinit is accepted and ignored - this runtime is garbage collected, so there is
// no deterministic point at which to run it.
DeinitDecl  <~~ push({}) ~~> = KwDeinit SkipBlock ;
// subscript(i: Int) -> Int { get { ... } set { ... } } becomes the pair of ordinary
// methods __sub_get / __sub_set, which is what [i] reads and writes dispatch to. A
// second subscript of a different arity is an overload of the same pair.
SubscriptDecl <~~ var ms = makeSubscript(takeAll()); for (var i = 0; i < ms.length; i++) { push(ms[i]) } ~~>
            = KwSubscript [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] PropAccessors ;
MethodDecl  <~~ push({method: makeFun(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] FnBody ;
// var w = 2, h = 3 declares two stored properties; each binding pushes its own
// {prop} record, so the type builder sees them in declaration order.
PropDecl    = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd PropBind { "," PropBind } [ ";" ] ;
PropBind    <~~ push(makeProp(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" AnnType ] [ "=" Expression ] ;
// A computed property (`var scaled: Int { get { ... } set { ... } }`, or the
// getter-only shorthand `var family: String { "beast" }`) and a stored property
// with willSet/didSet observers - the two share one syntax, told apart by which
// accessors the braces hold. Both become the ordinary methods __get_x / __set_x on
// the type, which is what a member read/write of x is emitted as; an observed
// property keeps its storage under the hidden name __v_x.
ComputedProp <~~ push(makeComputed(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" AnnType ] [ "=" PropDefault ] PropAccessors ;
PropDefault <~~ push({pdefault: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
// The accessor list is tried first: `get` and `set` are ordinary identifiers, so a
// getter-only body would otherwise read `get { ... }` as a trailing-closure call.
// Each alternative carries its own braces, so a body that is NOT an accessor list
// makes the whole alternative fail and the getter shorthand is reached (the same
// shape FnBody uses - an ordered choice never retries a committed alternative).
// A brace run this grammar cannot read as accessors (a coroutine accessor's
// `_modify { yield &x }`) is still ACCEPTED structurally by the last alternative.
PropAccessors = AccessorBlock | ImplicitGetter | OpaqueAccessors ;
OpaqueAccessors <~~ push({}) ~~> = SkipBlock ;
AccessorBlock = "{" Accessor { Accessor } "}" ;
Accessor    <~~ push(makeAccessor(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] AccKind <~~ push({akind: up.in}) ~~> [ AccParam ] [ FnEffects ] AccBody ;
AccKind     = ( "willSet" | "didSet" | "get" | "set" | "_modify" | "_read"
              | "unsafeAddress" | "unsafeMutableAddress" ) KwEnd ;
AccParam    = "(" Id <~~ push({aparam: up.in}) ~~> ")" ;
AccBody     <~~ push({abody: pop()}) ~~> = FnBody ;
// `var family: String { "beast" }` - the braces ARE the getter body.
ImplicitGetter <~~ push(makeImplicitGetter(pop())) ~~> = FnBody ;

// An enum declares cases (bare, with a raw value, or with associated values) and,
// like a struct, methods / computed properties / static members. Its body is the
// SAME Member list a struct has plus CaseDecl.
EnumDecl    <~~ push(makeEnumStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwEnum Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" EnumConform ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
// The FIRST entry of an enum's inheritance clause may be its raw-value type
// (`: Int`, `: String`); anything else there is a protocol and is ignored.
EnumConform = EnumRawType { "," Type } ;
EnumRawType <~~ push({eraw: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
CaseDecl    = KwCase ECase { "," ECase } [ ";" ] ;
ECase       <~~ push({ecase: makeECase(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({cname: up.in}) ~~> [ ECaseAssoc | "=" ECaseRaw ] ;
ECaseAssoc  <~~ push({cassoc: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" [ ECaseParam { "," ECaseParam } ] ")" ;
ECaseParam  <~~ push(makeECaseParam(takeAll())) ~~> = [ ECaseLabel ] Type ;
ECaseLabel  = Id <~~ push({clabel: up.in}) ~~> ":" ;
ECaseRaw    <~~ push({craw: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;

// protocol / extension / typealias: accepted structurally, not implemented.
UnsupDecl   = ImportDecl | ProtocolDecl | ExtensionDecl | TypeAliasDecl ;
// import Geom (or a submodule import 'import struct A.B'): the module name is resolved by
// resolveImport - a builtin module (core.stdlibImports) is a no-op, otherwise it maps to a
// project file (Geom -> Geom.swift, A.B -> A/B.swift) whose top-level declarations are
// stashed by buildMain (importDepth > 0) and prepended to jsmain. The submodule kind is
// parsed and dropped. It pushes no statement, so buildMain never sees the import itself.
ImportDecl  <~~ resolveImport(pop(), up.pos) ~~>
            = KwImport [ ImportKind ] ImportPath [ ";" ] ;
ImportPath  <~~ push(stripWs(up.in)) ~~>
            = Id { "." ( Id | "*" ) } ;
ImportKind  = ( "typealias" | "struct" | "class" | "enum" | "protocol"
              | "func" | "let" | "var" | "inout" ) KwEnd ;
// A protocol is a named member table: its REQUIREMENTS are recorded and dropped
// (this front end does no conformance checking), and the default implementations an
// extension of the protocol adds are what a conforming type inherits.
ProtocolDecl <~~ push(makeProtocolStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwProtocol Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ SkipAngle ] [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ]
              "{" { ProtoMember } "}" ;
// A requirement has no body, so it is matched BEFORE the member forms: `var name:
// String { get }` would otherwise read as a computed property whose getter returns
// the value of a variable called `get`.
ProtoMember = ProtoReq | Member ;
ProtoReq    = AssocTypeDecl | [ MemberModifiers ] ( ProtoVar | ProtoFunc | ProtoInit | ProtoSubscript ) ;
AssocTypeDecl <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = "associatedtype" KwEnd Id [ ":" TypeList ] [ "=" Type ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoVar    <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Id [ ":" Type ] "{" ProtoAcc { ProtoAcc } "}" ;
ProtoAcc    = ( "get" | "set" | "async" | "throws" ) KwEnd ;
ProtoFunc   <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = KwFunc FuncName [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ "->" AnnType ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoInit   <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = KwInit [ "?" | "!" ] [ SkipAngle ] ParamList [ FnEffects ] [ GenWhere ] [ ";" ] ;
ProtoSubscript <~~ push({}) ~~>
            = KwSubscript [ SkipAngle ] SkipParen "->" Type "{" ProtoAcc { ProtoAcc } "}" ;

// extension T { ... } adds members to an existing type - a struct/class/enum, a
// protocol (whose members become the defaults of every conforming type) or a
// builtin (Int, String, Array, ...), which keeps its members in a side table.
ExtensionDecl <~~ push(makeExtensionStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwExtension ExtType [ ":" InheritList ] [ GenWhere ] "{" { Member } "}" ;
ExtType     <~~ push({extname: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// typealias: types are parsed and ignored, so an alias needs nothing at run time.
TypeAliasDecl <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~>
            = KwTypealias Id [ SkipAngle ] "=" Type [ ";" ] ;

// Types are parsed and ignored: arrays [T], dictionaries [K: V], optionals T?,
// implicitly unwrapped T!, tuples/function types (A, B) -> C, dotted and generic,
// opaque/existential `some P` / `any P`, and compositions P & Q. The `&` of a
// composition is spelled ( "&" !"&" ) so `x is T && y` is not read as `T & (&y)`.
Type        = TypeMod TypeSuffixed { TypeAmp TypeMod TypeSuffixed } ;
// The optional / implicitly-unwrapped marker has to be ADJACENT to the type:
// `x is T ? a : b` must keep its conditional, and `T?` never has a space in Swift.
TypeSuffixed = TypeCore { NoSpace ( "?" | "!" ) } ;
// A zero-width guard that fails when whitespace (or end of file) is ahead - the
// dialect skips whitespace before each token, so "no space here" has to be asked
// explicitly.
NoSpace     = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var cc = c.peek(0)
        return (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc < 0)
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01space-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
TypeAmp     = "&" !"&" ;
TypeMod     = { ( "some" | "any" | "inout" | "borrowing" | "consuming" | "each" ) KwEnd } ;
TypeCore    = "[" Type [ ":" Type ] "]"
            | "(" [ TupleTypeElem { "," TupleTypeElem } ] ")" [ TypeEffects ] [ "->" Type ]
            | Id [ SkipAngle ] { "." Id [ SkipAngle ] } ;
TupleTypeElem = [ TupleTypeLabel ] { ParamAttr } Type [ "..." ] ;
TupleTypeLabel = Id [ Id ] ":" ;

// The element names of a tuple live in its TYPE, not in the literal: for
//     let pair: (n: String, v: Int) = ("k", 9)
// pair.n has to work. AnnType is Type with that one extra job - when the
// annotation is a tuple type whose first element is labelled it pushes the label
// list, and the declaration (or the function return) copies those names onto the
// value. NotArrow declines a function type, whose parameter labels name nothing.
AnnType     = LabelledTupleType | Type ;
LabelledTupleType <~~ push({tlabels: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" LtNamed { "," LtElem } ")" NotArrow ;
LtElem      = LtNamed | LtAnon ;
LtNamed     = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ":" Type ;
LtAnon      <~~ push("") ~~> = Type ;
NotArrow    = !"->" ;
TypeEffects = { ( "async" | "throws" | "rethrows" ) KwEnd } ;

// Every statement production pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> block.
// do/throw are genuinely lowered (try/catch via js_try, throw via js_throw); the
// Cplx* / Local* / defer / fallthrough forms are recognized but not lowered - they
// route to notImplStmt (a warning under -warn-unsupported).
Statement   <~~ push(stmtPos(pop())) ~~>
            = PoundDirective
            | Decl | LocalFunc | LocalType | CplxIf | If | CplxGuard | Guard
            | CplxWhile | While | Repeat | For | Switch | Return | Throw | Break
            | Continue | Fallthrough | Do | Defer | Empty | TupleAssign
            | TrailCallStmt | ExprStmt ;

// `keep11 { 20 }` - a call of a plain function written with one trailing closure and
// no parentheses. It is a STATEMENT form rather than an expression form on purpose:
// as an expression, `cond { ... }` would swallow the block of `if cond { ... }`, and
// PEG never goes back to re-read the condition. Every keyword-led statement is
// matched before this rule, so only a bare `name { ... }` reaches it.
TrailCallStmt <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~> = TrailCall [ ";" ] ;
TrailCall   <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> TrailArg { LabTrail } ;

// A loop (or switch) label: `outer: for ... { ... break outer }`. It is part of the
// loop production rather than a statement of its own, so the builder sees its own
// label and a labelled break/continue can name the loop it leaves.
LoopLabel   = Id <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> ":" ;

// A nested function or nested type declaration inside a body.
// A nested function declaration inside a body: a closure over the enclosing scope
// bound to its own name (so it can recurse and can capture surrounding locals).
LocalFunc   <~~ push(makeLocalFunStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = [ Modifiers ] FunDecl ;
// A nested type declaration inside a body - the same struct/class/enum productions
// as at the top level, run as a statement of the enclosing body.
LocalType   = StructDecl | ClassDecl | ActorDecl | EnumDecl ;

// do { STMTS } catch [pattern] { STMTS } [catch ...]: genuine try/catch (Swift has NO
// finally). throw calls js_throw (a Go panic the runtime unwinds); each body compiles
// to its own IR closure handed to js_try. The first catch clause wins (patterns are
// parsed but not discriminated). A return/break/continue leaving a try or catch body
// becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues in the enclosing frame. A catch
// with no `let NAME` binds the implicit name `error`. Each part is marked so makeTry
// can tell them apart. defer and fallthrough stay not lowered.
Do          <~~ push(makeTry(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwDo TryBody { CatchClause } ;
TryBody     <~~ push({trybody: pop()}) ~~>
            = Block ;
// catch PATTERN[, PATTERN] [where COND] { ... }. The clause matches when one of its
// patterns matches the thrown value (a clause with no pattern matches anything), and
// that pattern's bindings - plus the implicit `error` - are live in the body.
CatchClause <~~ push(makeCatchClause(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCatch [ CatchPat { "," CatchPat } [ CatchWhere ] ] Block ;
CatchPat    <~~ push({cpat: pop()}) ~~> = Pattern [ "as" KwEnd Type ] ;
CatchWhere  <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;
// defer { ... }: everything AFTER it in its block is emitted into a js_try whose
// FINALLY closure is the defer body, so it runs on the normal path, on a return (a
// control signal that excDispatch re-issues) and on a throw (js_try re-panics after
// the finally). Nested defers therefore unwind LIFO.
Defer       <~~ push(makeDefer(pop())) ~~>
            = KwDefer Block ;
Throw       <~~ push(makeThrow(pop())) ~~>
            = KwThrow Expression [ ";" ] ;
// fallthrough: continue into the NEXT case's body without re-testing its pattern.
Fallthrough <~~ push(makeFallthrough()) ~~>
            = KwFallthrough [ ";" ] ;

// if / guard / while whose condition uses optional binding (if let x = ...),
// pattern matching (if case ...) or a compound comma condition. Plain boolean
// conditions fall through to If/Guard/While, which stay the cheaper single-test
// forms; a clause reaches here only when it carries a comma or a binding. Each
// condition item is emitted in turn and branches to the failure block; the names a
// binding declares live in a child scope for if/while and, Swift-style, in the
// ENCLOSING scope for guard (whose else branch is expected to leave that scope).
CplxIf      <~~ push(makeCondIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf CplxCondClause Block [ KwElse ElseBody ] ;
CplxGuard   <~~ push(makeCondGuard(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwGuard CplxCondClause KwElse Block ;
CplxWhile   <~~ push(makeCondWhile(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwWhile CplxCondClause Block ;
// A complex clause requires either a comma (2+ conditions) or a leading binding.
CplxCondClause <~~ push({conds: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = CondItem "," CondItem { "," CondItem } | BindCond { "," CondItem } ;
CondItem    = BindCond | CondExpr ;
CondExpr    <~~ push({cexpr: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
BindCond    = OptBind | CaseCond ;
// `if let x = e` and the 5.7 shorthand `if let x` (which rebinds x from itself).
OptBind     <~~ push(makeOptBind(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd BindPat [ ":" Type ] [ "=" CondInit ] [ PatWhere ] ;
CaseCond    <~~ push(makeCaseCond(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwCase Pattern "=" CondInit [ PatWhere ] ;
CondInit    <~~ push({cinit: pop()}) ~~> = Expression ;
PatWhere    <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;
BindPat     <~~ push({bnames: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" BindName { "," BindName } ")" | BindName ;
BindName    <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;

// A pattern: `_`, `let x`, `x?` (optional pattern), a tuple of patterns, or an
// enum case - `.busy`, `Load19.busy`, optionally with a sub-pattern argument list.
// A bare identifier is a BINDING when it sits under a `let`/`var` and an enum case
// reference otherwise, so emitMatchCore carries that flag down the tree.
Pattern     <~~ push(makePattern(takeAll())) ~~>
            = PatCore { NoSpace "?" <~~ push({popt: 1}) ~~> } ;
PatCore     = PatLet | PatTuple | PatWild | PatRange | PatEnum | PatExpr ;
// Range patterns, including the one-sided forms: 1...9, 10..., ..<0. Tried before
// PatEnum and PatExpr, both of which would match the lower bound on its own and
// then leave the range operator behind.
PatRange    = PatRangeCC | PatRangeUp | PatRangeDn ;
PatRangeCC  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = PatOperand RangeOp PatOperand ;
PatRangeUp  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = PatOperand RangeOp ;
PatRangeDn  <~~ push(makePatRange(takeAll())) ~~> = RangeOp PatOperand ;
// A value pattern compares the subject with ==. Its operand is deliberately NOT the
// full expression grammar: a `{ ... }` would be read as a closure and swallow the
// body of `catch { ... }`, whose pattern is optional.
PatExpr     <~~ push({pexpr: pop()}) ~~> = PatOperand ;
PatOperand  = PatNegLit | Template | FloatLit | IntLit | True | False | Nil ;
PatNegLit   <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~> = "-" ( FloatLit | IntLit ) ;
PatLet      <~~ push({plet: pop()}) ~~> = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Pattern ;
PatTuple    <~~ push({ptup: takeAll()}) ~~> = "(" Pattern { "," Pattern } ")" ;
PatWild     <~~ push({pwild: 1}) ~~> = Wildcard ;
PatEnum     <~~ push(makePatEnum(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ "." <~~ push({pdot: 1}) ~~> ] PatPath [ PatArgs ] ;
PatPath     <~~ push({ppath: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Id { "." Id } ;
PatArgs     <~~ push({pargs: takeAll()}) ~~> = "(" [ PatArg { "," PatArg } ] ")" ;
PatArg      = [ PatArgLabel ] Pattern ;
PatArgLabel = Id ":" ;
Wildcard    = "_" KwEnd ;

Block       <~~ push(makeBlockStmt(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" { PoundIfStmt | Statement } "}" ;

Decl        = DeclTuple | DeclPlain ;
DeclPlain   <~~ push(makeDecl(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" AnnType ] [ "=" Expression ] [ ";" ] ;
// let (a, _, c) = someTuple - a tuple-pattern declaration; _ discards its element.
DeclTuple   <~~ push(makeDeclTuple(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( "let" | "var" ) KwEnd TupNames [ ":" Type ] "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;
TupNames    <~~ push({tnames: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "(" TupName { "," TupName } ")" ;
TupName     <~~ push(up.in) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;
// (x, y) = (y, x) - assignment through a tuple pattern (a swap, typically).
TupleAssign <~~ push(makeTupleAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = TupNames "=" Expression [ ";" ] ;

If          <~~ push(makeIf(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwIf Expression Block [ KwElse ElseBody ] ;
ElseBody    = Block | CplxIf | If ;

While       <~~ push(makeWhileL(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwWhile Expression Block ;

Repeat      <~~ push(makeRepeat(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwRepeat Block KwWhile Expression [ ";" ] ;

// `for case PAT in xs` filters: only the elements the pattern matches run the body
// (and the pattern's `let` names are bound for that iteration). Tried first - its
// `case` would otherwise be read as the loop variable's name by ForRange/ForList.
For         = ForCase | ForRange | ForList ;
ForCase     <~~ push(makeForCase(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor KwCase Pattern KwIn Expression [ ForWhere ] Block ;
ForRange    <~~ push(makeForRange(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor ForPat KwIn Expression RangeOp Expression [ ForWhere ] Block ;
RangeOp     = "..." <~~ push({rop: "..."}) ~~>
            | "..<" <~~ push({rop: "..<"}) ~~> ;
ForList     <~~ push(makeForList(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwFor ForPat KwIn Expression [ ForWhere ] Block ;
// The loop variable: a name, _ , or a tuple pattern (for (k, v) in aDictionary).
ForPat      = TupNames | ForName ;
ForName     <~~ push({fname: up.in}) ~~> = Wildcard | Id ;
// for x in xs where COND { ... }: the body runs only for the elements that pass.
ForWhere    <~~ push({fwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;

// guard COND else { ... }: a false condition branches into the else block (which
// normally diverges via return/break/continue); a true condition falls through.
Guard       <~~ push(makeGuard(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwGuard Expression KwElse Block ;

// switch SUBJ { case V1, V2: ...  default: ... } - a chain of == tests, no
// fallthrough. Body parsed FLAT (labels before Statement, a bare 'case' is a valid
// identifier); the builder groups statements under the label they follow. break in
// a case leaves the switch (loop stack), continue reaches the enclosing loop.
Switch      <~~ push(makeSwitch(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ LoopLabel ] KwSwitch SwitchSubj "{" { PoundIfCase | CaseLabel | Statement } "}" ;
SwitchSubj  <~~ push({subj: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression ;
// A case label is a comma-separated list of PATTERNS with an optional where guard:
// values (case 1, 2), ranges (case 1...9, case ..<0), tuples (case (0, let y)),
// enum cases with their associated values (case .rect(let w, let h)) and bindings
// (case let (x, y) where x == y).
CaseLabel   <~~ push(makeCaseLabel(takeAll())) ~~>
            = ( KwCase CaseItem { "," CaseItem } [ CaseWhere ]
              | KwDefault ) ":" ;
CaseItem    <~~ push({cpat: pop()}) ~~> = Pattern ;
CaseWhere   <~~ push({cwhere: pop()}) ~~> = KwWhere Expression ;

Return      <~~ push(makeReturn(takeAll())) ~~>
            = KwReturn [ Expression ] [ ";" ] ;
Break       <~~ push(makeBreakL(takeAll())) ~~>    = KwBreak [ SameLine BreakLbl ] [ ";" ] ;
Continue    <~~ push(makeContinueL(takeAll())) ~~> = KwContinue [ SameLine BreakLbl ] [ ";" ] ;
BreakLbl    <~~ push({lbl: up.in}) ~~> = Id ;

// A zero-width guard that fails at end of line (a newline or a // comment before the
// next token): the label of `break outer` must start on the SAME line, otherwise a
// bare `break` followed by a statement would swallow that statement's first name.
SameLine    = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var i = 0
        var cc = c.peek(i)
        while (cc === 32 || cc === 9) { i++; cc = c.peek(i) }
        return (cc === 10 || cc === 13 || cc < 0 || (cc === 47 && c.peek(i + 1) === 47))
            ? abnf.newToken("\x01newline-follows", 0)
            : undefined
    })()
~~) ;
Empty       <~~ push(function(b) { return b }) ~~> = ";" ;

ExprStmt    <~~ push(exprToStmt(pop())) ~~>
            = Expression [ ";" ] ;

// Every expression production pushes exactly one thunk: function(block) -> {b, v}.
Expression  = Assignment | Ternary ;
Assignment  <~~ push(makeAssign(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Target ( "+=" | "-=" | "*=" | "/=" | "%=" | "=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Expression ;

Target      <~~ push(makeTargetRef(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> { "." Id <~~ push({key: up.in}) ~~> | TIndex } ;
TIndex      <~~ push({idx: makeIndexKey(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," Expression } "]" ;

Ternary     <~~ push(makeTernary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = OrExpr [ "?" Expression ":" Ternary ] ;
OrExpr      <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), true)) ~~>
            = AndExpr { "||" AndExpr } ;
AndExpr     <~~ push(makeOrAnd(takeAll(), false)) ~~>
            = EqExpr { "&&" EqExpr } ;
// === / !== compare object identity; the three-character forms are listed first
// so `a === b` is not read as `a == (= b)`.
EqExpr      <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = RelExpr { ( "===" | "!==" | "==" | "!=" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> RelExpr } ;
RelExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Cast { ( "<=" | ">=" | "<" | ">" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> Cast } ;
// e as T / e as? T / e as! T / e is T: recognized, routed to notImplExpr. With no
// cast operator it is a pure pass-through (a single item), so byte output is
// unchanged for programs that do not cast.
Cast        <~~ push(makeCast(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = Coalesce { CastOp CastType } ;
CastOp      <~~ push({cast: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = ( "as" KwEnd [ "?" | "!" ] | "is" KwEnd ) ;
CastType    <~~ push({ctype: stripWs(up.in)}) ~~> = Type ;
// a ?? b yields a unless it is nil (the nil-coalescing operator; b only runs then).
Coalesce    <~~ push(makeCoalesce(takeAll())) ~~>
            = AddExpr { "??" AddExpr } ;
AddExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = MulExpr { ( "+" | "-" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> MulExpr } ;
MulExpr     <~~ push(foldBinary(takeAll())) ~~>
            = UnaryExpr { ( "*" | "/" | "%" ) <~~ push(up.in) ~~> UnaryExpr } ;

UnaryExpr   = Neg | Not | TryExpr | Await | CallMember ;
Neg         <~~ push(makeNeg(pop())) ~~>   = "-" UnaryExpr ;
Not         <~~ push(makeNot(pop())) ~~>   = "!" UnaryExpr ;
// try / try? / try! : the prefix is parsed and ignored and the inner expression is
// lowered normally (a throw inside the call unwinds via js_throw regardless).
TryExpr     = TryOpt | TryPlain ;
TryOpt      <~~ push(makeTryOpt(pop())) ~~> = KwTry "?" UnaryExpr ;
TryPlain    = KwTry [ "!" ] UnaryExpr ;
// await: recognized as a prefix, routed to notImplExpr.
Await       <~~ pop(); push(notImplExpr("await expression", up.pos)) ~~>
            = KwAwait UnaryExpr ;

CallMember  <~~ push(foldCallMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Primary { OptSfx | ForceSfx | MethodSfx | FieldSfx | TupleSfx | IndexSfx | CallSfx } ;
// x!: force-unwrap. Adjacent, and not the start of a != operator. The "not an =
// next" test has to be a script and cannot be !"=" - a literal in this dialect
// skips leading whitespace, so !"=" in `maybe! == 7` looks PAST the space, finds
// the = of == and rejects a force-unwrap that is perfectly fine.
ForceSfx    <~~ push({kind:"force"}) ~~> = NoSpace "!" NotChar61 ;
// x?.y / x?[i]: optional chaining - a nil anywhere along the chain makes the WHOLE
// chain nil instead of failing. Adjacent, and not the start of ?? or a ternary.
OptSfx      <~~ push({kind:"opt"}) ~~> = NoSpace "?" NotChar63 ;
NotChar61   = :script(~~
    (function() { return (c.peek(0) === 61) ? abnf.newToken("\x01eq-follows", 0) : undefined })()
~~) ;
NotChar63   = :script(~~
    (function() { return (c.peek(0) === 63) ? abnf.newToken("\x01q-follows", 0) : undefined })()
~~) ;
MethodSfx   <~~ push({kind:"mcall", name: popName(), args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({mname: up.in}) ~~> MArgs ;
FieldSfx    = "." Id <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~> ;
// t.0 / t.1: a tuple element read. A tuple is an object whose elements live under
// the property names "0", "1", ... (and additionally under their label when the
// element has one), so an element read is an ordinary field read.
TupleSfx    = "." TupleIdx ;
TupleIdx    <~~ push({kind:"field", name: up.in}) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;
IndexSfx    <~~ push({kind:"index", idx: makeIndexKey(takeAll())}) ~~>
            = "[" Expression { "," Expression } "]" ;
// f(args) applied to whatever the chain produced so far - a closure out of an array
// (queued[0]()), a function returned by a function, a computed member. The opening
// paren has to be ADJACENT: `print(x)` on one line and `(a, b) = (b, a)` on the
// next must stay two statements, not a call of the first one's result.
CallSfx     <~~ push({kind:"call", args: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NoSpaceParen "(" [ CallArg { "," CallArg } ] ")" ;
NoSpaceParen = :script(~~
    (function() {
        return (c.peek(0) === 40) ? undefined : abnf.newToken("\x01space-before-paren", 0)
    })()
~~) ;
MArgs       = "(" [ CallArg { "," CallArg } ] ")" [ NoTrailCtx TrailArg { LabTrail } ]
            | NoTrailCtx TrailArg { LabTrail } ;
// A zero-width guard that FAILS when the `{` ahead is the BODY of an
// if / while / switch / for / guard rather than a trailing closure. Swift resolves
// this ambiguity with a parser flag ("no trailing closure in a condition"); a guard
// here has to re-derive the same answer from (source, position) alone, so it walks
// BACKWARDS to the start of the enclosing statement - tracking bracket depth, and
// answering "allowed" the moment it leaves an unclosed ( or [ , which is what makes
// `check(xs.map { $0 })` inside a condition still work - and looks at the first word
// it finds there. Without it, `if case .p = E.c(3) { body }` reads `{ body }` as a
// trailing closure of E.c(3) and the if has no block left.
NoTrailCtx  = :script(~~
    (function() {
        var j = 0
        var ch = c.peek(j)
        while (ch === 32 || ch === 9 || ch === 10 || ch === 13) { j++; ch = c.peek(j) }
        if (ch !== 123) { return undefined }
        var depth = 0
        var i = -1
        var steps = 0
        var start = 0
        var found = false
        while (steps < 20000) {
            steps++
            var b = c.peek(i)
            if (b < 0) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
            if (b === 41 || b === 93 || b === 125) { depth++ }
            else if (b === 40 || b === 91) {
                if (depth === 0) { return undefined }
                depth--
            } else if (b === 123) {
                if (depth === 0) { start = i + 1; found = true; break }
                depth--
            } else if (depth === 0 && (b === 59 || b === 10 || b === 13)) {
                start = i + 1; found = true; break
            }
            i--
        }
        if (!found) { return undefined }
        var k = start
        var w = ""
        var rounds = 0
        while (rounds < 4) {
            rounds++
            var cc = c.peek(k)
            while (cc === 32 || cc === 9 || cc === 10 || cc === 13) { k++; cc = c.peek(k) }
            w = ""
            while ((cc >= 97 && cc <= 122) || (cc >= 65 && cc <= 90)) {
                w = w + String.fromCharCode(cc); k++; cc = c.peek(k)
            }
            if (w !== "else") { break }
        }
        if (w === "if" || w === "while" || w === "switch" || w === "for" || w === "guard") {
            return abnf.newToken("\x01trailing-closure-in-condition", 0)
        }
        return undefined
    })()
~~) ;
// A trailing closure is the callee's last argument; the second and further ones
// carry their argument label (Swift 5.3 multiple trailing closures).
TrailArg    <~~ push(makeTrailArg(pop())) ~~> = Closure ;
LabTrail    <~~ push(makeLabTrail(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push({alabel: up.in}) ~~> ":" Closure ;

// An argument carries its label (matched against the parameter labels of the
// callee, so a call may leave defaulted parameters out) and, for &x, the name it
// passes by reference.
CallArg     <~~ push(makeCallArg(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ ArgLabel ] ( InoutArg | Expression ) ;
ArgLabel    = Id <~~ push({alabel: up.in}) ~~> ":" ;
// &x: an inout argument. Spelled ( "&" !"&" ) so it cannot start on the first
// character of a && operator.
InoutArg    = "&" !"&" Id <~~ push({aref: up.in}) ~~> ;

Primary     = Paren | Template | FloatLit | IntLit | True | False | Nil
            | PoundExpr | SuperRef | DotMember | Closure | DictLit | ArrayLit
            | Shorthand | MultiTrail | Call | VarRef ;

// `super.m(...)` / `super.p` / `super.init(...)`: a marker value whose member lookup
// starts at the SUPERCLASS of the type the running method was declared in, with the
// same self. (The declaring type is what makes a three-level hierarchy behave: the
// lookup base is not self's own type.)
SuperRef    <~~ push(makeSuperRef()) ~~> = KwSuper ;

// An implicit member expression: `.tails`, `.circle(r: 5)`. Swift resolves the
// leading dot against the expected TYPE; this front end has no type checker, so it
// builds an unqualified enum case value carrying only the case name and, if given,
// its associated values. Matching and == compare enum cases by NAME, so an
// unqualified `.tails` and a qualified `Coin06.tails` are the same value.
DotMember   <~~ push(makeDotMember(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "." Id <~~ push({dname: up.in}) ~~> [ DotArgs ] ;
DotArgs     <~~ push({dargs: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = NoSpaceParen "(" [ CallArg { "," CallArg } ] ")" ;

// The # expression literals. #available / #unavailable answer a constant (this
// runtime has no platform to compare against, and the guarded code is the code
// we want to run); the rest are recognized and reported as not implemented.
// Longest first: "#file" would otherwise match the front of "#fileID".
PoundExpr   = PoundAvail | PoundUnavail | PoundCallLit | PoundBareLit ;
PoundAvail  <~~ push(makeConst(true)) ~~>  = "#available" SkipParen ;
PoundUnavail <~~ push(makeConst(false)) ~~> = "#unavailable" SkipParen ;
PoundCallLit <~~ push(notImplExpr("# expression", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "#selector" | "#keyPath" | "#colorLiteral" | "#imageLiteral"
              | "#fileLiteral" | "#externalMacro" ) SkipParen ;
PoundBareLit <~~ push(notImplExpr("# literal", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "#fileID" | "#filePath" | "#file" | "#function" | "#line"
              | "#column" | "#dsohandle" ) KwEnd ;

// ( e ) is a pass-through; ( e, e, ... ) is a tuple, recognized and routed to
// notImplExpr. Element labels (x: e) are accepted and dropped.
Paren       <~~ push(makeParen(takeAll(), up.pos)) ~~>
            = "(" TupleElem { "," TupleElem } ")" ;
TupleElem   <~~ push(makeTupleElem(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ TupleLabel ] Expression ;
TupleLabel  = Id <~~ push({tlabel: up.in}) ~~> ":" ;
// Floating-point literals (3.14): recognized, routed to notImplExpr (the runtime
// is 32-bit integer only). Tried before IntLit; a plain integer or a range (1...5)
// still parses as IntLit because a fractional digit is required.
FloatLit    <~~ push(notImplExpr("floating-point literal", up.pos)) ~~>
            = ( "0" | "1"..."9" ) :whitespace() { "0"..."9" } "." "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
// Closure shorthand arguments ($0, $1, ...): recognized, routed to notImplExpr.
// Closure shorthand arguments ($0, $1, ...): a closure without a signature binds
// its arguments under exactly these names, so the shorthand is an ordinary read.
Shorthand   = "$" :whitespace() ShortIdx :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
ShortIdx    <~~ push(makeVarRef("$" + up.in)) ~~>
            = "0"..."9" { "0"..."9" } ;

Closure     <~~ push(makeClosure(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "{" [ ClosureSig ] { PoundIfLam | LamStmt } "}" ;
ClosureSig  <~~ push(makeClosureSig(takeAll())) ~~>
            = [ CaptureList ] "(" [ ClParam { "," ClParam } ] ")" [ FnEffects ] [ "->" Type ] KwIn
            | [ CaptureList ] ClParam { "," ClParam } KwIn
            | CaptureList KwIn ;
ClParam     = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ ":" Type ] ;
// { [x, weak y] in ... }: a capture list snapshots the named variables at the
// moment the closure is created.
CaptureList <~~ push({caps: takeAll()}) ~~>
            = "[" [ CapItem { "," CapItem } ] "]" ;
CapItem     = [ CapMode ] Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> [ "=" DiscardExpr ] ;
CapMode     = ( "weak" | "unowned" ) KwEnd ;
LamStmt     = LamCtrl | LamExpr ;
LamCtrl     <~~ push({stmt: pop()}) ~~>
            = Decl | While | Repeat | For | Return | Break | Continue | If ;
LamExpr     <~~ push({expr: pop()}) ~~>
            = Expression [ ";" ] ;

DictLit     <~~ push(makeDict(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" ( ":" | DictEntry { "," DictEntry } ) "]" ;
DictEntry   <~~ var v = pop(); push({dk: pop(), dv: v}) ~~>
            = Expression ":" Expression ;
ArrayLit    <~~ push(makeArray(takeAll())) ~~>
            = "[" [ Expression { "," Expression } ] "]" ;

Call        <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> "(" [ CallArg { "," CallArg } ] ")" [ NoTrailCtx TrailArg { LabTrail } ] ;
// f { ... } label: { ... }: a call written with two or more trailing closures and no
// parentheses. Only the MULTI-closure form is accepted without parentheses - a bare
// `name { ... }` would make `if cond { ... }` parse as a call of cond.
MultiTrail  <~~ push(makeCall(takeAll())) ~~>
            = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> TrailArg LabTrail { LabTrail } ;

// String interpolation: "sum = \(a + b)".
Template    <~~ push(makeTemplate(takeAll())) ~~>
            = '"' :whitespace() { TplExpr | TplChunk } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
TplExpr     <~~ push({e: pop()}) ~~>
            = "\\(" :whitespace(Whitespace) Expression ")" ;
TplChunk    <~~ push({s: unescapeJs(up.in)}) ~~>
            = TplChar { TplChar } ;
TplChar     = TplEsc | TplPlain ;
TplEsc      = "\\" ( "n" | "t" | "r" | "0" | "\\" | @'"' | "'" ) ;
TplPlain    = "\x20"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\x7e" | @"\t" | "\x7f"..."\U0010ffff" ;

Nil         <~~ push(makeConst(null)) ~~>  = "nil" KwEnd ;
VarRef      <~~ push(makeVarRef(up.in)) ~~> = Id ;

// ----- Widened surface: a balanced { ... } / < ... > run consumed but ignored -----
SkipBlock   = "{" { SkipItem } "}" ;
SkipItem    = SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipBlock | SkipOther ;
SkipStr     = '"' :whitespace() { SkipStrEsc | SkipStrChar } '"' :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipStrEsc  = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipStrChar = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChar    = "'" :whitespace() { SkipChEsc | SkipChChar } "'" :whitespace(Whitespace) ;
SkipChEsc   = "\\" "\x00"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipChChar  = "\x00"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x5b" | "\x5d"..."\U0010ffff" ;
SkipOther   = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x28"..."\x7a" | "\x7c" | "\x7e"..."\U0010ffff" ;

SkipAngle   = "<" { SkipAngle | SkipAngleItem } ">" ;
SkipAngleItem = "\x00"..."\x3b" | "\x3d" | "\x3f"..."\U0010ffff" ;

// A balanced ( ... ) run consumed but ignored (attribute args, subscript params).
SkipParen   = "(" { SkipStr | SkipChar | SkipParen | SkipPOther } ")" ;
SkipPOther  = "\x00"..."\x21" | "\x23"..."\x26" | "\x2a"..."\U0010ffff" ;

DiscardExpr <~~ pop() ~~> = Expression ;

KwFunc      = "func" KwEnd ;
// A function name is an identifier or, for an operator declaration, the operator.
FuncName    = Id <~~ push(up.in) ~~> ;
KwImport    = "import" KwEnd ;
KwWhere     = "where" KwEnd ;
KwDeinit    = "deinit" KwEnd ;
KwSubscript = "subscript" KwEnd ;
KwDo        = "do" KwEnd ;
KwCatch     = "catch" KwEnd ;
KwDefer     = "defer" KwEnd ;
KwThrow     = "throw" KwEnd ;
KwFallthrough = "fallthrough" KwEnd ;
KwTry       = "try" KwEnd ;
KwSuper     = "super" KwEnd ;
KwAwait     = "await" KwEnd ;
KwStruct    = "struct" KwEnd ;
KwClass     = "class" KwEnd ;
KwEnum      = "enum" KwEnd ;
KwCase      = "case" KwEnd ;
KwInit      = "init" KwEnd ;
KwProtocol  = "protocol" KwEnd ;
KwExtension = "extension" KwEnd ;
KwTypealias = "typealias" KwEnd ;
KwIf        = "if" KwEnd ;
KwGuard     = "guard" KwEnd ;
KwSwitch    = "switch" KwEnd ;
KwDefault   = "default" KwEnd ;
KwElse      = "else" KwEnd ;
KwWhile     = "while" KwEnd ;
KwRepeat    = "repeat" KwEnd ;
KwFor       = "for" KwEnd ;
KwIn        = "in" KwEnd ;
KwReturn    = "return" KwEnd ;
KwBreak     = "break" KwEnd ;
KwContinue  = "continue" KwEnd ;

// ---


:startScript(~~

    // Shared emitter machinery (module, handle constants, externs, loopStack
    // protocol, expression/statement builders); see the `core` knobs below.
    include("lib/compile-core.js")

    var hidCount = 0     // Names hidden loop bounds.
    var classNames = {}  // Known struct/class names (constructor calls).

    function fail(msg) { println("swift compiler error: " + msg); exit(1) }

    // ----- Imports -----
    // System frameworks / modules the platform already provides (the standard library,
    // Foundation and the common Apple frameworks, plus the platform C libraries);
    // importing one is a no-op. Anything else is a project module we resolve to a file.
    core.stdlibImports = ["Swift", "Foundation", "Dispatch", "Combine", "SwiftUI", "UIKit",
                          "AppKit", "CoreGraphics", "CoreData", "CoreFoundation", "ObjectiveC",
                          "Darwin", "Glibc"]

    // Project-file imports: 'import Geom' looks for Geom.swift (a dotted 'import A.B'
    // for A/B.swift, then A.swift) under the program's directory and the -i roots. The
    // file is parsed with this grammar; its top-level declarations are stashed by
    // buildMain (importDepth > 0) and prepended to jsmain. A file is imported once;
    // repeats and cycles are no-ops.
    var importedFiles = {}
    var importDepth = 0
    // imported files top-level items: stashImported / emitImported (compile-core)
    core.importFile = function(path, pos) {
        var rel = path.split(".").join("/")
        var cands = [rel + ".swift"]
        var cut = -1
        for (var k = 0; k < rel.length; k++) { if (rel.charAt(k) == "/") { cut = k } }
        if (cut > 0) { cands.push(rel.substring(0, cut) + ".swift") }
        for (var i = 0; i < cands.length; i++) {
            var file = c.findImport(cands[i])
            if (file == "") { continue }
            if (importedFiles["$" + file] == true) { return true }
            importedFiles["$" + file] = true
            var text = c.readFile(file)
            var asg = c.parse(c.agrammar, text, null, file)
            c.pushSource(file, text)
            importDepth = importDepth + 1
            c.compile(asg)
            importDepth = importDepth - 1
            c.popSource()
            return true
        }
        return false
    }

    // ----- try/catch and throw (Swift has no finally) -----
    // Each do/catch body compiles to its own IR closure handed to js_try (jsrt.go): it
    // runs the try under a Go recover and routes a thrown value to the catch closure (a
    // one-parameter function bound to the catch name). The first catch clause wins.
    // makeCatch is shared - see lib/compile-core.js.
    // items = [{cpat}..., {cwhere}?, bodyThunk]
    function makeCatchClause(items) {
        var pats = []
        var whereT = null
        var body = null
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            var it = items[i]
            if (it == undefined) { continue }
            if (typeof it == "function") { body = it }
            else if (it.cpat != undefined) { pats.push(it.cpat) }
            else if (it.cwhere != undefined) { whereT = it.cwhere }
        }
        return {catchbody: body, pats: pats, whereT: whereT}
    }
    // The whole catch chain becomes ONE catch closure taking `error`: it tests each
    // clause's patterns in turn and runs the first that matches, and re-throws when
    // none does so an outer do still sees the error. Swift has no finally.
    function makeTry(items) {
        var tryT = anytype
        var clauses = []
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
            if (items[i].trybody != undefined) { tryT = items[i].trybody }
            else if (items[i].catchbody != undefined) { clauses.push(items[i]) }
        }
        var catchBody = null
        if (clauses.length > 0) {
            catchBody = function(b) {
                var errV = callExt(b, "js_kget", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, "error")])
                var rethrowB = curF.NewBlock("")
                var doneB = curF.NewBlock("")
                for (var k = 0; k < clauses.length; k++) {
                    var cl = clauses[k]
                    var runB = curF.NewBlock("")
                    var nextB = curF.NewBlock("")
                    if (cl.pats.length == 0) {
                        b.NewBr(runB)
                    } else {
                        for (var q = 0; q < cl.pats.length; q++) {
                            var tryNext = curF.NewBlock("")
                            var okB = emitMatchPat(b, cl.pats[q], errV, false, tryNext)
                            if (cl.whereT != null) {
                                var rw = cl.whereT(okB)
                                rw.b.NewCondBr(truthy(rw.b, rw.v), runB, tryNext)
                            } else { okB.NewBr(runB) }
                            b = tryNext
                        }
                        b.NewBr(nextB)
                    }
                    cl.catchbody(runB).NewBr(doneB)
                    b = nextB
                }
                // No clause matched: keep unwinding so an outer do still sees it.
                b.NewBr(rethrowB)
                callExt(rethrowB, "js_throw", [errV])
                rethrowB.NewBr(doneB)
                return doneB
            }
        }
        return function(b) {
            // The try and catch bodies are ctl closures (isCtl), so a return/break/
            // continue leaving them becomes a control signal that excDispatch re-issues.
            var tryClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc([], tryT, false, true)), curScopeV])
            var catchClo = hUndef
            if (catchBody != null) {
                catchClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc(["error"], catchBody, false, true)), curScopeV])
            }
            var ctl = callExt(b, "js_try", [tryClo, catchClo, hUndef])
            return excDispatch(b, ctl)
        }
    }
    // try? EXPR: nil instead of the thrown error. The operand becomes a closure so
    // js_try can wrap it, and its value is the result of the whole expression.
    function makeTryOpt(t) {
        return function(b) {
            var bodyFn = function(bb) {
                var r = t(bb)
                r.b.NewRet(r.v)
                return deadBlock()
            }
            var nilFn = function(bb) {
                bb.NewRet(hNull)
                return deadBlock()
            }
            var bodyClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc([], bodyFn, false, false)), curScopeV])
            var catchClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc(["error"], nilFn, false, false)), curScopeV])
            return {b: b, v: callExt(b, "js_try", [bodyClo, catchClo, hUndef])}
        }
    }
    // throw e: evaluate e and raise it with js_throw (a Go panic the runtime unwinds).
    function makeThrow(e) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = e(b)
            callExt(r.b, "js_throw", [r.v])
            r.b.NewRet(hUndef) // Never reached: js_throw unwinds on the host side.
            return deadBlock()
        }
    }

    // ----- Expression thunks: function(block) -> {b, v} -----

    // e as/as?/as! T and e is T carry a {cast:1} marker per operator. With no
    // marker the single operand thunk passes through unchanged; a cast is not
    // lowered (the runtime has no dynamic types), so it becomes a placeholder.
    // e is T answers the dynamic type test (js_is_type, the Go twin of the
    // interpreter's rtIsType), e as? T the value or nil, and e as / e as! T the value
    // itself - the value model has one representation per value, so an upcast and a
    // forced downcast are identities. An enum case answers to its enum's name.
    function makeCastOne(t, op, tname) {
        if (op != "is" && op != "as?") { return t }
        return function(b) {
            var r = t(b)
            b = r.b
            var isB = callExt(b, "js_is_type", [r.v, emitStr(b, tname)])
            var elseB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var joinB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var fromB = b
            b.NewCondBr(truthy(b, isB), joinB, elseB)
            // A case value's type is its enum, which js_is_type does not know about.
            var nm = elseB.NewCall(rtProbe("sw_etype", "__etype"), [r.v])
            var alt = callExt(elseB, "js_seq", [nm, emitStr(elseB, tname)])
            elseB.NewBr(joinB)
            var hit = joinB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hTrue, fromB), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(alt, elseB)])
            if (op == "is") { return {b: joinB, v: hit} }
            var yB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var nB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var mB = curF.NewBlock("")
            joinB.NewCondBr(truthy(joinB, hit), yB, nB)
            yB.NewBr(mB)
            nB.NewBr(mB)
            return {b: mB, v: mB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(r.v, yB), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hNull, nB)])}
        }
    }
    function makeCast(items, pos) {
        if (items.length == 1) { return items[0] }
        var t = items[0]
        for (var i = 1; i + 1 < items.length; i = i + 2) {
            t = makeCastOne(t, items[i].cast, items[i + 1].ctype)
        }
        return t
    }

    // ----- Tuples -----
    // A tuple is a plain object carrying its elements under the property names
    // "0", "1", ... plus, for a labelled element, under its label - so t.0 and
    // t.lo are both ordinary js_get reads and the runtime needs no new type.
    function makeTupleElem(items) {
        if (items.length > 1) { return {lbl: items[0].tlabel, t: items[1]} }
        return {lbl: null, t: items[0]}
    }
    // ( e ) passes the single unlabelled operand through; anything else is a tuple.
    function makeParen(items, pos) {
        if (items.length == 1 && items[0].lbl == null) { return items[0].t }
        return function(b) {
            var t = callExt(b, "js_obj_new", [])
            callExt(b, "js_set", [t, emitStr(b, "__tuple"), emitNum(b, items.length)])
            for (var i = 0; i < items.length; i++) {
                var r = items[i].t(b)
                b = r.b
                callExt(b, "js_set", [t, emitStr(b, "" + i), r.v])
                if (items[i].lbl != null) { callExt(b, "js_set", [t, emitStr(b, items[i].lbl), r.v]) }
            }
            return {b: b, v: t}
        }
    }
    // Copies the element names of a tuple TYPE onto a tuple value (the names of a
    // tuple live in its type, so `let p: (n: String, v: Int) = ("k", 9)` has to
    // make p.n work).
    function emitRelabel(b, v, labels) {
        for (var i = 0; i < labels.length; i++) {
            if (labels[i] == "") { continue }
            callExt(b, "js_set", [v, emitStr(b, labels[i]), callExt(b, "js_get", [v, emitStr(b, "" + i)])])
        }
        return v
    }
    // let (a, _, c) = t  /  (x, y) = (y, x): bind or assign element by element.
    function makeDeclTuple(items) {
        var names = items[0].tnames
        var init = items[1]
        return function(b) {
            var r = init(b)
            b = r.b
            for (var i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {
                if (names[i] == "_") { continue }
                var ev = callExt(b, "js_get", [r.v, emitStr(b, "" + i)])
                callExt(b, "js_scope_decl", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, names[i]), ev])
            }
            return b
        }
    }
    function makeTupleAssign(items) {
        var names = items[0].tnames
        var init = items[1]
        return function(b) {
            var r = init(b)
            b = r.b
            for (var i = 0; i < names.length; i++) {
                if (names[i] == "_") { continue }
                var ev = callExt(b, "js_get", [r.v, emitStr(b, "" + i)])
                callExt(b, "js_kset", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, names[i]), ev])
            }
            return b
        }
    }

    // A body that is a single expression returns that expression's value.
    function makeExprBody(t) {
        return function(b) {
            var r = t(b)
            var v = retLabelled(r.b, r.v)
            emitInoutBacks(r.b)
            return retStmt(r.b, v)
        }
    }

    // ----- Loop control with labels -----
    // loopStack entries carry the label of their loop (null when unlabelled); a
    // labelled break/continue branches to the named entry rather than the top one.
    function loopFind(lbl) {
        for (var i = loopStack.length - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            if (loopStack[i].lbl === lbl) { return loopStack[i] }
        }
        return null
    }
    function makeBreakL(items) {
        if (items.length == 0) { return makeBreak() }
        var lbl = items[0].lbl
        return function(b) {
            var e = loopFind(lbl)
            if (e != null) { b.NewBr(e.brk) } else { emitBreak(b) }
            return deadBlock()
        }
    }
    function makeContinueL(items) {
        if (items.length == 0) { return makeContinue() }
        var lbl = items[0].lbl
        return function(b) {
            var e = loopFind(lbl)
            if (e != null) { b.NewBr(e.cont) } else { emitContinue(b) }
            return deadBlock()
        }
    }
    // Is this value a dictionary? js_keys aborts on a string or a number, so the
    // test has to be "an object that is not an array" rather than a bare js_keys.
    function emitIsDict(bb, v) {
        var isArr = callExt(bb, "js_is_type", [v, emitStr(bb, "Array")])
        var chkB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var joinB = curF.NewBlock("")
        var fromB = bb
        bb.NewCondBr(truthy(bb, isArr), joinB, chkB)
        var isObj = callExt(chkB, "js_seq", [callExt(chkB, "js_typeof", [v]), emitStr(chkB, "object")])
        chkB.NewBr(joinB)
        var phi = joinB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(hFalse, fromB), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(isObj, chkB)])
        return {b: joinB, v: phi}
    }
    function itemLbl(items) {
        if (items.length > 0 && items[0] != undefined && items[0].lbl != undefined) { return items[0].lbl }
        return null
    }
    function itemsAfterLbl(items) {
        if (itemLbl(items) != null) { return items.slice(1) }
        return items
    }
    // Binds one iteration value to the loop pattern (a name, _ or a tuple pattern).
    function emitBindForPat(b, pat, v) {
        if (pat.tnames != undefined) {
            for (var i = 0; i < pat.tnames.length; i++) {
                if (pat.tnames[i] == "_") { continue }
                callExt(b, "js_scope_decl", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, pat.tnames[i]),
                                             callExt(b, "js_get", [v, emitStr(b, "" + i)])])
            }
            return
        }
        if (pat.fname != "_") { callExt(b, "js_scope_decl", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, pat.fname), v]) }
    }

    // ----- defer -----
    // A defer is not a statement thunk but a MARKER in its block's item list; the
    // block builder below wraps the rest of the block in a js_try with the defer body
    // as its finally clause. A defer that was never reached - an early return above
    // it - never runs, exactly like Swift's.
    function makeDefer(t) { return {isdefer: t} }
    function swSeqWithDefers(items) {
        var cut = -1
        for (var i = 0; i < items.length && cut < 0; i++) {
            if (typeof items[i] != "function") { cut = i }
        }
        if (cut < 0) { return makeSeq(items) }
        var before = makeSeq(items.slice(0, cut))
        var deferT = items[cut].isdefer
        var restT = swSeqWithDefers(items.slice(cut + 1))
        return function(b) {
            b = before(b)
            // The rest of the block is a ctl closure (isCtl), so a return/break/
            // continue leaving it becomes a signal that excDispatch re-issues here.
            var bodyClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc([], restT, false, true)), curScopeV])
            var finClo = callExt(b, "js_closure", [handle(emitFunc([], deferT, false, false)), curScopeV])
            return excDispatch(b, callExt(b, "js_try", [bodyClo, hUndef, finClo]))
        }
    }
    // Both overridden by ASSIGNMENT (see the include-override trap in the gotchas doc).
    makeBlockStmt = function(items) {
        var seq = swSeqWithDefers(items)
        return function(b) {
            var saved = curScopeV
            curScopeV = callExt(b, "js_scope_new", [curScopeV])
            b = seq(b)
            curScopeV = saved
            return b
        }
    }
    // A defer marker is a RECORD, not a thunk, so the source-position wrapper the
    // Statement rule applies (active only under -trace / -cfgraph) has to pass it
    // through untouched for the block builder to still recognize it.
    var coreStmtPos = stmtPos
    stmtPos = function(t) {
        if (typeof t != "function") { return t }
        return coreStmtPos(t)
    }

    // An unqualified name inside a member body may be a computed property or a
    // method of the enclosing type: js_kget only sees the scope chain and the
    // receiver's OWN properties. When the name is one some type computes (or one some
    // type declares as a method) and no local of that name is in scope, the read is a
    // dispatch on self instead. Names no type uses this way keep the plain js_kget.
    var coreMakeVarRef = makeVarRef
    makeVarRef = function(name) {
        var plain = coreMakeVarRef(name)
        return function(b) {
            if (!isComputedName(name) && !isMemberMethod(name)) { return plain(b) }
            var t = callExt(b, "js_scope_typeof", [curScopeV, emitStr(b, name)])
            var localB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var selfB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var joinB = curF.NewBlock("")
            b.NewCondBr(truthy(b, callExt(b, "js_seq", [t, emitStr(b, "undefined")])), selfB, localB)
            var lv = callExt(localB, "js_kget", [curScopeV, emitStr(localB, name)])
            localB.NewBr(joinB)
            var selfV = callExt(selfB, "js_kget", [selfB == null ? curScopeV : curScopeV, emitStr(selfB, "self")])
            var sv
            if (isComputedName(name)) { sv = selfB.NewCall(rtGetProp(), [selfV, emitStr(selfB, name)]) }
            else { sv = callExt(selfB, "js_get", [selfV, emitStr(selfB, name)]) }
            selfB.NewBr(joinB)
            return {b: joinB, v: joinB.NewPhi([llvm.ir.NewIncoming(lv, localB), llvm.ir.NewIncoming(sv, selfB)])}
        }
    }

    // ----- Emitted runtime helpers -----
    // A handful of routines are too branchy to inline at every use site, so they are
    // emitted ONCE as ordinary IR functions and called directly. rtFunc registers the
    // function before emitting its body, so a helper may call itself.
    var rtFuncs = {}
    function rtFunc(name, nparams, bodyFn) {
        if (hasOwn(rtFuncs, "$" + name)) { return rtFuncs["$" + name] }
        var ps = []
        for (var i = 0; i < nparams; i++) { ps.push(llvm.ir.NewParam("p" + i, i64)) }
        var f = m.NewFunc(name, i64, ps)
        rtFuncs["$" + name] = f
        var savedF = curF
        var savedScope = curScopeV
        var savedLoops = loopStack
        var savedTag = curFTag
        curF = f
        loopStack = []
        var b = f.NewBlock("entry")
        bodyFn(b, f.Params)
        curF = savedF
        curScopeV = savedScope
        loopStack = savedLoops
        curFTag = savedTag
        return f
    }
    // A guarded property read: undefined for anything that is not a plain object, so
    // it is safe to apply to a value of unknown type (js_get aborts on nil).
    function rtProbe(name, prop) {
        return rtFunc(name, 1, function(b, ps) {
            var v = ps[0]
            var noB = curF.NewBlock("")
            var c1 = curF.NewBlock("")
            var c2 = curF.NewBlock("")
            var c3 = curF.NewBlock("")
            b.NewCondBr(truthy(b, callExt(b, "js_seq", [v, hNull])), noB, c1)
            c1.NewCondBr(truthy(c1, callExt(c1, "js_seq", [v, hUndef])), noB, c2)
